        { type: "intuition", title: "The receptionist who opens the letter",
          html: "<p><b>Analogy.</b> An ALB is a receptionist who opens each letter, reads it, and walks it to the right department: invoices to accounts, CVs to HR. That is slower than a ZIP-code sorter, but far smarter.</p>" +
                "<p><b>Definition (SL-L12 p10).</b> An Application Load Balancer works at the <b>application layer (Layer 7)</b>. It understands HTTP requests, URLs, headers, cookies and host names. Its listeners are <b>HTTP or HTTPS</b>. <b>Listener rules</b> match conditions such as path (/api/*), host (api.example.com), header or query string, and <b>forward</b> to a <b>target group</b>. An ALB has no static IP; clients reach it through a <b>DNS name</b>. At Layer 7 the ALB opens a <i>new</i> TCP connection to the target, so the target sees the ALB's IP. The client's IP travels in the <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> header.</p>" },
        { type: "figure", caption: "SL-L12 p10 and WB-L04 p19 redrawn. ALB path (left): Layer 7, splits on the URL path. NLB path (right): Layer 4, forwards everything to one target group.",
          html: "<svg viewBox='0 0 640 300' width='100%'><defs><marker id='u12m2' viewBox='0 0 10 10' refX='9' refY='5' markerWidth='7' markerHeight='7' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z' fill='currentColor'/></marker></defs><text x='160' y='20' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='var(--accent)'>Client requests</text><line x1='160' y1='26' x2='160' y2='48' stroke='var(--accent)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><rect x='60' y='50' width='200' height='44' rx='8' fill='none' stroke='var(--accent)' stroke-width='2'/><text x='160' y='70' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='currentColor'>Application load balancer</text><text x='160' y='86' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>Layer 7 · HTTP/HTTPS</text><line x1='130' y1='94' x2='90' y2='130' stroke='var(--accent)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><line x1='190' y1='94' x2='230' y2='130' stroke='var(--accent)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><text x='70' y='118' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--accent)'>/api/*</text><text x='255' y='118' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--accent)'>/web/*</text><rect x='40' y='132' width='100' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--accent)'/><text x='90' y='154' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>TG: api</text><rect x='180' y='132' width='100' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--accent)'/><text x='230' y='154' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>TG: web</text><rect x='40' y='196' width='100' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--muted)'/><text x='90' y='218' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>EC2 (3)</text><rect x='180' y='196' width='100' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--muted)'/><text x='230' y='218' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>EC2 (3)</text><line x1='90' y1='166' x2='90' y2='194' stroke='var(--accent)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><line x1='230' y1='166' x2='230' y2='194' stroke='var(--accent)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><text x='160' y='262' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--muted)'>No static IP: DNS name only</text><text x='480' y='20' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='var(--ok)'>Client requests</text><line x1='480' y1='26' x2='480' y2='48' stroke='var(--ok)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><rect x='380' y='50' width='200' height='44' rx='8' fill='none' stroke='var(--ok)' stroke-width='2'/><text x='480' y='70' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='currentColor'>Network load balancer</text><text x='480' y='86' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>Layer 4 · TCP/UDP/TLS</text><line x1='480' y1='94' x2='480' y2='130' stroke='var(--ok)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><text x='540' y='118' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--ok)'>forward all traffic</text><rect x='410' y='132' width='140' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--ok)'/><text x='480' y='154' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>Target group: tcp-app</text><line x1='480' y1='166' x2='480' y2='194' stroke='var(--ok)' marker-end='url(#u12m2)'/><rect x='410' y='196' width='140' height='34' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='var(--muted)'/><text x='480' y='218' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>EC2 instances (3)</text><text x='480' y='262' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--muted)'>Static IP + preserves client IP</text></svg>" },
        { type: "table", head: ["Rule priority", "Condition", "Action"],
          rows: [ ["1", "path is /api/*", "forward to target group TG-api"],
                  ["2", "path is /web/*", "forward to target group TG-web"],
                  ["3", "host is admin.example.com", "forward to target group TG-admin"],
                  ["default", "nothing else matched", "fixed response 404 (or forward to a default group)"] ],
          caption: "An HTTPS:443 listener with its rules. Rules are checked in priority order and the first match wins; this is what Unit12_l7_proxy.py implements." },
        { type: "callout", kind: "slidefix", title: "SL-L12 p11: the &ldquo;ALB&rdquo; console shows a TCP listener",
          html: "<p><b>Slide shows:</b> under the title &ldquo;Application Load Balancer (ALB)&rdquo;, a console screenshot with Listener <b>TCP:80</b> and a target group of type &ldquo;Instance, <b>TCP</b>&rdquo; (2 targets on port 80, registration in progress).</p><p><b>Correct:</b> TCP listeners and TCP target groups belong to a <b>Network</b> Load Balancer. An ALB's listeners are only <b>HTTP or HTTPS</b> (for example HTTP:80 and HTTPS:443), and its target groups use protocol HTTP or HTTPS (or gRPC). The screenshot is an NLB (compare p9, which shows the same layout under the NLB title).</p>" },
        { type: "figure", caption: "SL-L12 p12: TLS termination. The ALB holds the certificate (issued by AWS Certificate Manager), performs the TLS handshake and decryption, and forwards plain HTTP to the backend inside the VPC.",
          html: "<svg viewBox='0 0 640 130' width='100%'><defs><marker id='u12m3' viewBox='0 0 10 10' refX='9' refY='5' markerWidth='7' markerHeight='7' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z' fill='currentColor'/></marker></defs><rect x='10' y='40' width='110' height='50' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='currentColor'/><text x='65' y='70' text-anchor='middle' font-size='13' fill='currentColor'>Client</text><line x1='120' y1='65' x2='248' y2='65' stroke='var(--ok)' stroke-width='3' marker-end='url(#u12m3)'/><text x='184' y='55' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='var(--ok)'>HTTPS :443</text><text x='184' y='84' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--ok)'>encrypted</text><rect x='250' y='30' width='160' height='70' rx='8' fill='none' stroke='var(--accent)' stroke-width='2'/><text x='330' y='54' text-anchor='middle' font-size='13' fill='currentColor'>ALB</text><text x='330' y='72' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='currentColor'>TLS termination</text><text x='330' y='88' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--muted)'>ACM certificate</text><line x1='410' y1='65' x2='518' y2='65' stroke='var(--warn)' stroke-width='3' marker-end='url(#u12m3)'/><text x='464' y='55' text-anchor='middle' font-size='12' fill='var(--warn)'>HTTP :80</text><text x='464' y='84' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='var(--warn)'>inside the VPC</text><rect x='520' y='40' width='110' height='50' rx='6' fill='none' stroke='currentColor'/><text x='575' y='70' text-anchor='middle' font-size='13' fill='currentColor'>Backend</text></svg>" },
        { type: "callout", kind: "slidefix", title: "SL-L12 p13: ALB &ldquo;TCP/UDP: No&rdquo;",
          html: "<p><b>Slide shows:</b> in the NLB vs ALB table, the row &ldquo;TCP/UDP&rdquo; reads NLB Yes, ALB <b>No</b>.</p><p><b>Correct reading:</b> an ALB offers no <b>TCP or UDP listeners</b>, so you cannot load-balance a raw TCP or UDP protocol (a game server, DNS, MQTT) with it. HTTP and HTTPS still <b>run over TCP</b>: every ALB connection is a TCP connection with a handshake and congestion control. It is wrong to say an ALB &ldquo;does not use TCP&rdquo;.</p>" },
        { type: "table", head: ["Feature (SL-L12 p13)", "NLB", "ALB", "Why"],
          rows: [
            ["Layer", "4", "7", "NLB reads the 4-tuple; ALB parses HTTP"],
            ["TCP/UDP", "Yes", "No", "ALB has only HTTP/HTTPS listeners (which ride on TCP)"],
            ["HTTP aware", "No", "Yes", "Only ALB parses requests"],
            ["URL routing", "No", "Yes", "Path rules such as /api/* need the URL"],
            ["Header inspection", "No", "Yes", "Host, cookie, custom header and query-string rules"],
            ["TLS termination", "Limited", "Yes", "NLB can terminate on a TLS listener but still cannot read HTTP; ALB terminates on HTTPS listeners"],
            ["Source IP preservation", "Yes", "Usually via headers", "ALB opens its own connection to the target; client IP goes in X-Forwarded-For"],
            ["Latency", "Lowest", "Slightly higher", "Parsing HTTP and terminating TLS cost time"]
          ],
          caption: "The 8-row table from SL-L12 p13, with a reason for every row." },
        { type: "callout", kind: "aws", title: "Addresses and headers",
          html: "<p>An <b>ALB</b> is reached through a DNS name (my-alb-123456.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com) whose IPs can change, so Route 53 points at it with an <b>alias record</b>. An <b>NLB</b> gets one static IP per enabled AZ, optionally an Elastic IP, which suits firewall allow-lists. The ALB adds <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> (client IP), <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> (http or https) and <code>X-Forwarded-Port</code> to every request it forwards.</p>" },
        { type: "cheat", title: "ALB",
          items: [ "Layer 7; listeners HTTP:80 / HTTPS:443 only; routes on path, host, header, query string",
                   "Target groups: TG-api, TG-web (SL-L12 p10); first matching rule wins",
                   "TLS termination: HTTPS from client → ALB (ACM certificate) → HTTP to the target",
                   "DNS name only (no static IP); client IP in X-Forwarded-For",
                   "Choose NLB for raw TCP/UDP, a static IP, or extreme throughput; choose ALB for HTTP routing" ] },
        { type: "worked", title: "Pick the load balancer", tag: "University-Midsem-style",
          problem: "<p>For each workload choose NLB or ALB: (a) /api/* to a container fleet and /web/* to a static-site fleet; (b) a multiplayer game on UDP port 7777; (c) a partner whose firewall allows only fixed IPs; (d) HTTPS with certificates managed centrally, so that backends handle only HTTP.</p>",
          steps: [
            { text: "(a) ALB: path routing needs the URL, a Layer 7 field.", why: "NLB cannot read URLs (SL-L12 p9)." },
            { text: "(b) NLB: UDP needs a UDP listener.", why: "ALB has no TCP/UDP listeners (p13)." },
            { text: "(c) NLB: it has a static IP per AZ (Elastic IP possible).", why: "ALB has a DNS name only (p10)." },
            { text: "(d) ALB: HTTPS listener with an ACM certificate, forwarding HTTP.", why: "TLS termination (p12)." } ],
          answer: "<b>(a) ALB, (b) NLB, (c) NLB, (d) ALB</b>" },
        { type: "code", file: "Unit12_l7_proxy.py", level: "high", title: "A real L7 load balancer on 127.0.0.1: path routing, round robin, health checks",
          note: "A <code>ThreadingHTTPServer</code> proxy routes /api/* to TG-api (api-1 and a second instance, api-2) and /web/* to TG-web (web-1), all <code>http.server</code> backends. It adds X-Forwarded-For, health-checks GET /health, drops api-2 when it returns 500 and re-adds it when it recovers. It is all driven by <code>http.client</code> with asserts." },
        { type: "traps",
          items: [ "An ALB terminates the client's TCP connection. The target sees the <b>ALB's</b> IP; the client IP is only in X-Forwarded-For.",
                   "&ldquo;ALB: TCP/UDP No&rdquo; does not mean HTTP over an ALB avoids TCP.",
                   "SL-L12 p11's console is an NLB configuration (TCP:80 listener).",
                   "An ALB has no fixed IP. Point DNS at its name with an alias record, never at an IP you looked up once." ] }
